major":"Architecture","group":"Angkor","stem":"Angkor was a major centre of which civilization?","choices":["Minoan","Aztec","Khmer","Achaemenid","Inca"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Khmer","explanation":"Angkor combines temples, reservoirs and urban infrastructure. Angkor Wat and the Bayon belong to different building campaigns; the long range describes the city, not one temple.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/668/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Angkor","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Angkor was a major centre of which civilization?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-01"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-01"],"fingerprint":"12c9c49d3ceab1a1ac56c71a"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-002","qid":106002,"nodeId":"FACT106-002","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-002"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Angkor","stem":"What makes reservoirs important to understanding Angkor?","choices":["They prove the city had no permanent population","They connect temples with water management and urban infrastructure","They were all built as Greek theatres","They replaced every religious building","They show that the city was a Roman naval base"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"They connect temples with water management and urban infrastructure","explanation":"Angkor combines temples, reservoirs and urban infrastructure. Angkor Wat and the Bayon belong to different building campaigns; the long range describes the city, not one temple.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/668/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Angkor","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"What makes reservoirs important to understanding Angkor?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-01"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-01"],"fingerprint":"aa8f900481628ddf716a1676"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-003","qid":106003,"nodeId":"FACT106-003","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-003"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Taj Mahal","stem":"What was the original purpose of the Taj Mahal?","choices":["A mausoleum for Mumtaz Mahal","A Buddhist university","A military arsenal","A royal summer residence","A coronation theatre"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"A mausoleum for Mumtaz Mahal","explanation":"Shah Jahan commissioned this mausoleum for Mumtaz Mahal. Read the white marble tomb together with its garden and symmetrical complex; it is a funerary monument, not a royal residence.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/252/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Taj Mahal","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"What was the original purpose of the Taj Mahal?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-02"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-02"],"fingerprint":"ef24b9793b2e01b5454686a7"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-004","qid":106004,"nodeId":"FACT106-004","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-004"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Taj Mahal","stem":"Which ruler commissioned the Taj Mahal?","choices":["Shah Jahan","Ashoka","Darius I","Qin Shi Huang","Akbar"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Shah Jahan","explanation":"Shah Jahan commissioned this mausoleum for Mumtaz Mahal. Read the white marble tomb together with its garden and symmetrical complex; it is a funerary monument, not a royal residence.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/252/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Taj Mahal","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which ruler commissioned the Taj Mahal?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-02"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-02"],"fingerprint":"c658858f96ea0713b14ceb4c"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-005","qid":106005,"nodeId":"FACT106-005","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-005"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Borobudur","stem":"Which religious tradition is central to Borobudur?","choices":["Buddhism","Zoroastrianism","Jainism","Christianity","Shinto"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Buddhism","explanation":"A Buddhist monument with square terraces below circular platforms and a crowning stupa. The ascending route organizes reliefs and repeated stupas into a spatial sequence.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/592/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Borobudur","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which religious tradition is central to Borobudur?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-03"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-03"],"fingerprint":"7ad4b5b899121def2bf86602"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-006","qid":106006,"nodeId":"FACT106-006","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-006"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Borobudur","stem":"Which spatial sequence characterizes Borobudur?","choices":["A minaret surrounding a Renaissance dome","A nave ending in a Gothic chevet","An axial garden ending in a Mughal tomb","A Roman arena enclosed by tiered seats","Square terraces below circular platforms and a stupa"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":"Square terraces below circular platforms and a stupa","explanation":"A Buddhist monument with square terraces below circular platforms and a crowning stupa. The ascending route organizes reliefs and repeated stupas into a spatial sequence.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/592/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Borobudur","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which spatial sequence characterizes Borobudur?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-03"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-03"],"fingerprint":"1a7baa819cce69a58fd521ba"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-007","qid":106007,"nodeId":"FACT106-007","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-007"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Petra","stem":"Petra is especially associated with which ancient people?","choices":["Maya","Minoans","Khmer","Nabataeans","Etruscans"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":"Nabataeans","explanation":"Nabataean Petra combined caravan commerce, rock-cut architecture and careful water management. Carved facades do not mean that every part of the city was cut into rock.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/326/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Petra","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Petra is especially associated with which ancient people?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-04"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-04"],"fingerprint":"8490fdf3097edbf7c8f8d7ee"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-008","qid":106008,"nodeId":"FACT106-008","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-008"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Petra","stem":"Which pair best explains how Petra functioned as a desert city?","choices":["Caravan commerce and water management","Glacial storage and ski routes","Ocean shipping and tidal mills","Coal mines and steam railways","Rice paddies and monsoon canals"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Caravan commerce and water management","explanation":"Nabataean Petra combined caravan commerce, rock-cut architecture and careful water management. Carved facades do not mean that every part of the city was cut into rock.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/326/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Petra","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which pair best explains how Petra functioned as a desert city?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-04"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-04"],"fingerprint":"d329b00eb96a09bceb2f7c81"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-009","qid":106009,"nodeId":"FACT106-009","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-009"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Machu Picchu","stem":"Machu Picchu belongs to which civilization?","choices":["Maya","Inca","Moche","Olmec","Aztec"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"Inca","explanation":"The Inca site joins stone buildings, terraces and steep mountain terrain. Its fifteenth-century setting distinguishes it from earlier Andean cultures such as the Moche.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/274/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Machu Picchu","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Machu Picchu belongs to which civilization?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-05"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-05"],"fingerprint":"4a11a0ec39cc1c41e3434e18"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-010","qid":106010,"nodeId":"FACT106-010","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-010"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Machu Picchu","stem":"Which description best fits the setting of Machu Picchu?","choices":["Stone buildings and terraces integrated with steep Andean terrain","Temple stupas on a Javanese volcanic plain","Rock-cut facades beside a Jordanian caravan route","Imperial halls on a Beijing axis","Earthen mosques on a Sahelian plain"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Stone buildings and terraces integrated with steep Andean terrain","explanation":"The Inca site joins stone buildings, terraces and steep mountain terrain. Its fifteenth-century setting distinguishes it from earlier Andean cultures such as the Moche.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/274/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Machu Picchu","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which description best fits the setting of Machu Picchu?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-05"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-05"],"fingerprint":"44f8017729731799f1777d67"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-011","qid":106011,"nodeId":"FACT106-011","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-011"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Chichén Itzá","stem":"Chichén Itzá is located in which cultural-geographic setting?","choices":["The Iranian plateau","The Inca Andes","The Khmer heartland","The Nile valley","The Maya region of Yucatán"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":"The Maya region of Yucatán","explanation":"Temple pyramids, a large ball court and other monuments show a Maya city with wider Mesoamerican connections. Do not confuse it with Teotihuacan in central Mexico.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/483/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Chichén Itzá","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Chichén Itzá is located in which cultural-geographic setting?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-06"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-06"],"fingerprint":"092d986824c0e90daab13ec4"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-012","qid":106012,"nodeId":"FACT106-012","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-012"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Chichén Itzá","stem":"Which ensemble is associated with Chichén Itzá?","choices":["Temple pyramids and a large ball court","A Japanese keep and timber defensive gates","A Tibetan winter palace and Buddhist chapels","A Gothic nave and flying buttresses","A Roman forum and a triumphal arch"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Temple pyramids and a large ball court","explanation":"Temple pyramids, a large ball court and other monuments show a Maya city with wider Mesoamerican connections. Do not confuse it with Teotihuacan in central Mexico.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/483/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Chichén Itzá","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which ensemble is associated with Chichén Itzá?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-06"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-06"],"fingerprint":"475bc1b86eeea4145140960c"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-013","qid":106013,"nodeId":"FACT106-013","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-013"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Teotihuacan","stem":"Which statement correctly places Teotihuacan in time?","choices":["It flourished centuries before the Aztec Empire","It was a nineteenth-century reconstruction of Rome","It was founded by the Spanish conquistadors","It was founded as an Inca colonial capital","It was built after Mexican independence"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"It flourished centuries before the Aztec Empire","explanation":"The Avenue of the Dead links monumental precincts including the Sun and Moon pyramids. The city flourished many centuries before the Aztec Empire; its builders should not be labelled Aztec.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/414/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Teotihuacan","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which statement correctly places Teotihuacan in time?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-07"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-07"],"fingerprint":"988e2d1ab31ccd2112a4e8fb"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-014","qid":106014,"nodeId":"FACT106-014","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-014"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Teotihuacan","stem":"Which major route organizes the monumental core of Teotihuacan?","choices":["Avenue of the Dead","Royal Mile","Via Appia","Processional Way of Babylon","Champs-Élysées"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Avenue of the Dead","explanation":"The Avenue of the Dead links monumental precincts including the Sun and Moon pyramids. The city flourished many centuries before the Aztec Empire; its builders should not be labelled Aztec.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/414/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Teotihuacan","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which major route organizes the monumental core of Teotihuacan?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-07"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-07"],"fingerprint":"3d602da94e9586a82bf29ed8"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-015","qid":106015,"nodeId":"FACT106-015","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-015"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Rapa Nui: moai","stem":"The moai of Rapa Nui belong to which broad cultural world?","choices":["Mesopotamia","Scandinavia","Polynesia","Mesoamerica","The Sahel"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Polynesia","explanation":"The moai are monumental stone figures associated with a Polynesian island society. Many stand on ceremonial platforms called ahu. The range is a broad period, not a date for every statue.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/715/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Rapa Nui: moai","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"The moai of Rapa Nui belong to which broad cultural world?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-08"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-08"],"fingerprint":"9d3f5b7e72145e4371837c39"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-016","qid":106016,"nodeId":"FACT106-016","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-016"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Rapa Nui: moai","stem":"What is an ahu in the context of Rapa Nui?","choices":["A Buddhist manuscript cave","A ceremonial platform associated with moai","A Mughal water garden","A Roman heating system","A Chinese imperial examination hall"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"A ceremonial platform associated with moai","explanation":"The moai are monumental stone figures associated with a Polynesian island society. Many stand on ceremonial platforms called ahu. The range is a broad period, not a date for every statue.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/715/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Rapa Nui: moai","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"What is an ahu in the context of Rapa Nui?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-08"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-08"],"fingerprint":"e4555dbc22fa46ac6dd0863d"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-017","qid":106017,"nodeId":"FACT106-017","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-017"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Himeji Castle","stem":"Which structural material is fundamental to Himeji Castle?","choices":["Cast iron","Timber","Reinforced concrete","Entirely solid marble","Adobe throughout"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"Timber","explanation":"White plastered surfaces cover a sophisticated timber castle. Gates, walls and routes form a defensive system: its appearance is not simply ornamental.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/661/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Himeji Castle","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which structural material is fundamental to Himeji Castle?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-09"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-09"],"fingerprint":"e329e81d87457d0a8d396bf2"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-018","qid":106018,"nodeId":"FACT106-018","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-018"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Himeji Castle","stem":"Why should Himeji be read as more than an ornamental white building?","choices":["Its gates, walls and routes form a defensive system","It is a freestanding tomb without internal circulation","It is a natural cliff without constructed rooms","Its only purpose was astronomical observation","It was designed as an open-air Greek theatre"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Its gates, walls and routes form a defensive system","explanation":"White plastered surfaces cover a sophisticated timber castle. Gates, walls and routes form a defensive system: its appearance is not simply ornamental.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/661/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Himeji Castle","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Why should Himeji be read as more than an ornamental white building?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-09"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-09"],"fingerprint":"03908410fd955d9451bd4a75"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-019","qid":106019,"nodeId":"FACT106-019","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-019"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Forbidden City","stem":"Which dynasties used Beijing’s Forbidden City as an imperial palace?","choices":["Chola and Pandya","Achaemenid and Sasanian","Han and Tang","Maurya and Gupta","Ming and Qing"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":"Ming and Qing","explanation":"Beijing's imperial palace developed as a hierarchy of courts and halls along an axis. The initial Ming construction dates do not imply that every surviving structure is original.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/439/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Forbidden City","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which dynasties used Beijing’s Forbidden City as an imperial palace?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-10"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-10"],"fingerprint":"9a2ea5da3b893f426eca2e40"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-020","qid":106020,"nodeId":"FACT106-020","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-020"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Forbidden City","stem":"Which principle helps explain the plan of the Forbidden City?","choices":["An axial hierarchy of courtyards and halls","A spiral ramp around an industrial silo","A series of independent floating islands","A single circular arena for spectators","A nave and transept forming a Latin cross"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"An axial hierarchy of courtyards and halls","explanation":"Beijing's imperial palace developed as a hierarchy of courts and halls along an axis. The initial Ming construction dates do not imply that every surviving structure is original.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/439/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Forbidden City","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which principle helps explain the plan of the Forbidden City?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-10"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-10"],"fingerprint":"4c213dfbfce6ad674055ef4d"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-021","qid":106021,"nodeId":"FACT106-021","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-021"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Temple of Heaven","stem":"What was the Temple of Heaven primarily designed for?","choices":["Gladiatorial combat","Storage of modern industrial machinery","Imperial ceremonies relating heaven and earth","Naval shipbuilding","Private housing for merchants"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Imperial ceremonies relating heaven and earth","explanation":"This ceremonial complex relates imperial ritual to ideas of heaven and earth. Read its symbolism and layout as a religious-political landscape rather than a residential palace.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/881/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Temple of Heaven","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"What was the Temple of Heaven primarily designed for?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-11"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-11"],"fingerprint":"9075caf9404ecb2d8ca8e665"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-022","qid":106022,"nodeId":"FACT106-022","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-022"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Temple of Heaven","stem":"Which pairing correctly distinguishes two Beijing complexes?","choices":["Forbidden City: Roman forum; Temple of Heaven: amphitheatre","Forbidden City: observatory only; Temple of Heaven: merchant housing","Forbidden City: imperial palace; Temple of Heaven: ritual complex","Forbidden City: monastery in Tibet; Temple of Heaven: palace in Japan","Forbidden City: tomb; Temple of Heaven: shipyard"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Forbidden City: imperial palace; Temple of Heaven: ritual complex","explanation":"This ceremonial complex relates imperial ritual to ideas of heaven and earth. Read its symbolism and layout as a religious-political landscape rather than a residential palace.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/881/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Temple of Heaven","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which pairing correctly distinguishes two Beijing complexes?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-11"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-11"],"fingerprint":"385b025b499343dcd479062e"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-023","qid":106023,"nodeId":"FACT106-023","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-023"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Potala Palace","stem":"Potala Palace served as a winter residence of whom?","choices":["The Ottoman sultan","The Mughal emperor","The Dalai Lama","The pope","The Japanese shogun"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"The Dalai Lama","explanation":"The palace was a winter residence of the Dalai Lama and a centre of Tibetan Buddhist authority. Its major seventeenth-century development stands on a site with earlier history.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/707/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Potala Palace","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Potala Palace served as a winter residence of whom?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-12"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-12"],"fingerprint":"dadcc1f503e43105b9695e2a"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-024","qid":106024,"nodeId":"FACT106-024","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-024"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Potala Palace","stem":"Which cultural setting is essential to understanding Potala Palace?","choices":["Norse paganism","Roman civic religion","Minoan palace culture","Tibetan Buddhism","Aztec state ritual"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":"Tibetan Buddhism","explanation":"The palace was a winter residence of the Dalai Lama and a centre of Tibetan Buddhist authority. Its major seventeenth-century development stands on a site with earlier history.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/707/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Potala Palace","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which cultural setting is essential to understanding Potala Palace?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-12"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-12"],"fingerprint":"5f62699db4efe346997db08b"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-025","qid":106025,"nodeId":"FACT106-025","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-025"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Lalibela: rock-hewn churches","stem":"How were the celebrated medieval churches at Lalibela formed?","choices":["By suspending timber decks from cables","By casting concrete shells","By assembling prefabricated steel frames","By carving spaces and structures from rock","By inflating fabric membranes"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":"By carving spaces and structures from rock","explanation":"Eleven medieval churches were carved from rock and connected by trenches and passages. Excavating spaces and leaving structural masses produces architecture by subtraction.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/18/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Lalibela: rock-hewn churches","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"How were the celebrated medieval churches at Lalibela formed?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-13"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-13"],"fingerprint":"417a0b7057c9aa4b71c9dc2a"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-026","qid":106026,"nodeId":"FACT106-026","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-026"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Lalibela: rock-hewn churches","stem":"Which religious tradition is represented by Lalibela’s rock-hewn churches?","choices":["Japanese Shinto","Persian Zoroastrianism","Tibetan Buddhism","Ethiopian Christianity","Roman imperial cult"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":"Ethiopian Christianity","explanation":"Eleven medieval churches were carved from rock and connected by trenches and passages. Excavating spaces and leaving structural masses produces architecture by subtraction.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/18/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Lalibela: rock-hewn churches","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which religious tradition is represented by Lalibela’s rock-hewn churches?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-13"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-13"],"fingerprint":"79e0e2c0381a1728b5f5f367"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-027","qid":106027,"nodeId":"FACT106-027","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-027"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Great Zimbabwe","stem":"Great Zimbabwe is associated with which historical cultural context?","choices":["Roman occupation of Britain","Viking settlements in Iceland","Shona society and regional trade","Ming imperial administration in Beijing","Maya city-states in Yucatán"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Shona society and regional trade","explanation":"The ruins record a Shona urban and trading centre. Carefully fitted granite walls, especially the Great Enclosure, challenge the idea that monumental African architecture was limited to Egypt.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Great Zimbabwe","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Great Zimbabwe is associated with which historical cultural context?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-14"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-14"],"fingerprint":"80aca9eb8d258b1874fc8fc5"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-028","qid":106028,"nodeId":"FACT106-028","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-028"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Great Zimbabwe","stem":"Which material is characteristic of the monumental walls at Great Zimbabwe?","choices":["Bamboo","Reinforced concrete","Cast iron","Glazed porcelain","Granite"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":"Granite","explanation":"The ruins record a Shona urban and trading centre. Carefully fitted granite walls, especially the Great Enclosure, challenge the idea that monumental African architecture was limited to Egypt.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Great Zimbabwe","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which material is characteristic of the monumental walls at Great Zimbabwe?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-14"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-14"],"fingerprint":"27f32637eda9169b027c98e9"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-029","qid":106029,"nodeId":"FACT106-029","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-029"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Djenné: historic town","stem":"Which material tradition is central to the historic architecture of Djenné?","choices":["Earthen construction","Curtain-wall glass","Cast-iron framing","Prefabricated steel panels","Solid marble carving"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Earthen construction","explanation":"Earthen construction links the town's buildings to local materials and continuing maintenance. The range describes a historic urban phase, not the construction date of today's Great Mosque.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/116/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Djenné: historic town","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which material tradition is central to the historic architecture of Djenné?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-15"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-15"],"fingerprint":"afd5624315b23074020c25a5"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-030","qid":106030,"nodeId":"FACT106-030","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-030"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Djenné: historic town","stem":"Why must the history of Djenné be distinguished from the date of its present Great Mosque?","choices":["Every building in a historic town must have the same age","Earthen buildings can never be rebuilt","A town’s long history is not the construction date of each surviving building","A mosque can only be built in the ancient period","The presence of a mosque proves Roman authorship"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"A town’s long history is not the construction date of each surviving building","explanation":"Earthen construction links the town's buildings to local materials and continuing maintenance. The range describes a historic urban phase, not the construction date of today's Great Mosque.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/116/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Djenné: historic town","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Why must the history of Djenné be distinguished from the date of its present Great Mosque?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-15"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-15"],"fingerprint":"57fc80ac9a3a797466d008ca"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-031","qid":106031,"nodeId":"FACT106-031","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-031"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Persepolis","stem":"Persepolis was a monumental centre of which empire?","choices":["Achaemenid Persian Empire","Aztec Empire","Khmer Empire","Inca Empire","Carolingian Empire"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Achaemenid Persian Empire","explanation":"Darius I began the Achaemenid palace complex on a monumental terrace. Reliefs and ceremonial spaces expressed imperial authority; this is not a Greek polis.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/114/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Persepolis","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Persepolis was a monumental centre of which empire?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-16"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-16"],"fingerprint":"1fdd5b94511a21fc7156ed35"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-032","qid":106032,"nodeId":"FACT106-032","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-032"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Persepolis","stem":"Which ruler initiated the building of Persepolis around 518 BCE?","choices":["Justinian I","Darius I","Augustus","Alexander the Great","Shah Jahan"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"Darius I","explanation":"Darius I began the Achaemenid palace complex on a monumental terrace. Reliefs and ceremonial spaces expressed imperial authority; this is not a Greek polis.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/114/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Persepolis","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which ruler initiated the building of Persepolis around 518 BCE?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-16"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-16"],"fingerprint":"ddd5f8983740f712b52a3787"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-033","qid":106033,"nodeId":"FACT106-033","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-033"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Sanchi","stem":"In the context of Sanchi, what is a stupa?","choices":["A Chinese watchtower","A Buddhist sacred mound or reliquary monument","A Greek public theatre","A Mughal residential garden","A Roman apartment block"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"A Buddhist sacred mound or reliquary monument","explanation":"Stupas, gateways and other remains trace a long Buddhist history beginning in the Mauryan period. The stupa is a sacred mound or reliquary monument, not an ordinary dwelling.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/524/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Sanchi","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"In the context of Sanchi, what is a stupa?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-17"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-17"],"fingerprint":"7d803f9eea561c04718003e9"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-034","qid":106034,"nodeId":"FACT106-034","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-034"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Sanchi","stem":"The early Buddhist monuments at Sanchi reach back to which period?","choices":["The Qing dynasty","The Mauryan period","The Italian Renaissance","The European Baroque","The Industrial Revolution"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"The Mauryan period","explanation":"Stupas, gateways and other remains trace a long Buddhist history beginning in the Mauryan period. The stupa is a sacred mound or reliquary monument, not an ordinary dwelling.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/524/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Sanchi","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"The early Buddhist monuments at Sanchi reach back to which period?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-17"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-17"],"fingerprint":"904779983887ea3c703809d5"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-035","qid":106035,"nodeId":"FACT106-035","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-035"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Khajuraho","stem":"Which religious traditions are represented among the temples at Khajuraho?","choices":["Christianity and Shinto","Hinduism and Jainism","Zoroastrianism and Norse religion","Judaism and Roman imperial cult","Islam and ancient Egyptian religion"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"Hinduism and Jainism","explanation":"The surviving temples combine towered sanctuaries with extensive sculpture. Both Hindu and Jain monuments are represented; reducing the site to erotic imagery misses its wider religious programme.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/240/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Khajuraho","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which religious traditions are represented among the temples at Khajuraho?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-18"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-18"],"fingerprint":"8f542182079fd018a598a32f"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-036","qid":106036,"nodeId":"FACT106-036","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-036"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Khajuraho","stem":"Which dynasty is associated with the main temple-building phase at Khajuraho?","choices":["Achaemenid","Ptolemaic","Carolingian","Chandella","Ming"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":"Chandella","explanation":"The surviving temples combine towered sanctuaries with extensive sculpture. Both Hindu and Jain monuments are represented; reducing the site to erotic imagery misses its wider religious programme.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/240/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Khajuraho","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which dynasty is associated with the main temple-building phase at Khajuraho?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-18"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-18"],"fingerprint":"b5ce002c7e38d0ed2d433123"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-037","qid":106037,"nodeId":"FACT106-037","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-037"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Bagan","stem":"Bagan’s monument landscape is primarily associated with which religion?","choices":["Zoroastrianism","Judaism","Shinto","Christianity","Buddhism"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":"Buddhism","explanation":"Thousands of Buddhist monuments form a landscape shaped by patronage and merit-making. The selected range describes the principal building era, not every restoration.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1588/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Bagan","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Bagan’s monument landscape is primarily associated with which religion?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-19"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-19"],"fingerprint":"e16a147eeb3c048af104f272"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-038","qid":106038,"nodeId":"FACT106-038","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-038"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Bagan","stem":"Which activity helped shape Bagan’s dense landscape of temples?","choices":["Industrial railway construction","Roman gladiatorial spectacle","Atlantic naval warfare","Olympic stadium planning","Religious patronage and merit-making"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":"Religious patronage and merit-making","explanation":"Thousands of Buddhist monuments form a landscape shaped by patronage and merit-making. The selected range describes the principal building era, not every restoration.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1588/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Bagan","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which activity helped shape Bagan’s dense landscape of temples?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-19"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-19"],"fingerprint":"b74827ba74d862984938b4c1"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-039","qid":106039,"nodeId":"FACT106-039","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-039"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Samarkand","stem":"Samarkand’s major fourteenth- and fifteenth-century monuments are especially associated with which dynasty?","choices":["Ming","Julio-Claudian","Chola","Ptolemaic","Timurid"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":"Timurid","explanation":"Samarkand links Silk Road exchange with Timurid monuments and scholarship. The Registan, Bibi-Khanum Mosque and Ulugh Beg's observatory have different functions and building histories.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/603/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Samarkand","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Samarkand’s major fourteenth- and fifteenth-century monuments are especially associated with which dynasty?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-20"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-20"],"fingerprint":"604388a8e83f61bbc16cfdbb"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-040","qid":106040,"nodeId":"FACT106-040","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-040"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Samarkand","stem":"What was the function of Ulugh Beg’s observatory at Samarkand?","choices":["Astronomical observation","Gladiatorial combat","Naval ship repair","Imperial burial","Ceramic firing"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Astronomical observation","explanation":"Samarkand links Silk Road exchange with Timurid monuments and scholarship. The Registan, Bibi-Khanum Mosque and Ulugh Beg's observatory have different functions and building histories.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/603/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Samarkand","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"What was the function of Ulugh Beg’s observatory at Samarkand?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-20"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-20"],"fingerprint":"4ec2ad0760b043147bc70fa7"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-041","qid":106041,"nodeId":"FACT106-041","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-041"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Hampi","stem":"Hampi was the capital of which southern Indian kingdom?","choices":["Vijayanagara","The Khmer kingdom","Ming China","The Inca state","Achaemenid Persia"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Vijayanagara","explanation":"The former Vijayanagara capital brings together temples, royal structures and a dramatic rocky landscape. The ruins belong to a major southern Indian kingdom, not to Mughal Agra.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/241/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Hampi","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Hampi was the capital of which southern Indian kingdom?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-21"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-21"],"fingerprint":"244cae813540203b4ede9c5e"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-042","qid":106042,"nodeId":"FACT106-042","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-042"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Hampi","stem":"Which pairing correctly locates Hampi and the Taj Mahal in different historical settings?","choices":["Hampi: Inca citadel; Taj Mahal: Maya ball court","Hampi: Gothic cathedral; Taj Mahal: Viking hall","Hampi: Ming palace; Taj Mahal: Shinto shrine","Hampi: Roman colony; Taj Mahal: Greek theatre","Hampi: Vijayanagara capital; Taj Mahal: Mughal mausoleum"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":"Hampi: Vijayanagara capital; Taj Mahal: Mughal mausoleum","explanation":"The former Vijayanagara capital brings together temples, royal structures and a dramatic rocky landscape. The ruins belong to a major southern Indian kingdom, not to Mughal Agra.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/241/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Hampi","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which pairing correctly locates Hampi and the Taj Mahal in different historical settings?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-21"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-21"],"fingerprint":"ba39964e19b8de622e489323"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-043","qid":106043,"nodeId":"FACT106-043","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-043"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Konark Sun Temple","stem":"The Sun Temple at Konark represents the chariot of which deity?","choices":["Amaterasu","Athena","Surya","Shiva","Quetzalcoatl"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Surya","explanation":"The temple gives architectural form to the chariot of Surya, the sun god. Carved wheels and horses turn an entire building into a religious image.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/246/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Konark Sun Temple","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"The Sun Temple at Konark represents the chariot of which deity?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-22"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-22"],"fingerprint":"d3e15fa9cefa1c96db391485"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-044","qid":106044,"nodeId":"FACT106-044","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-044"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Konark Sun Temple","stem":"Which carved elements make the chariot symbolism of Konark legible?","choices":["Pendentives and a lantern","Flying buttresses and gargoyles","Minarets and muqarnas","Wheels and horses","Triglyphs and metopes"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":"Wheels and horses","explanation":"The temple gives architectural form to the chariot of Surya, the sun god. Carved wheels and horses turn an entire building into a religious image.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/246/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Konark Sun Temple","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which carved elements make the chariot symbolism of Konark legible?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-22"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-22"],"fingerprint":"5a367edaa2cc6972a4053c67"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-045","qid":106045,"nodeId":"FACT106-045","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-045"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Mogao Caves","stem":"Mogao’s location near Dunhuang connects its Buddhist art to which exchange network?","choices":["The Hanseatic Baltic routes","The Inca road system","The Silk Road","The Roman limes in Britain","The Atlantic triangular trade"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"The Silk Road","explanation":"Wall paintings and sculpture preserve many centuries of Buddhist art along the Silk Road. The caves also connect art history with manuscripts and intercultural exchange.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/440/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Mogao Caves","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Mogao’s location near Dunhuang connects its Buddhist art to which exchange network?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-23"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-23"],"fingerprint":"7d2d3f27e0be97d78d9913c5"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-046","qid":106046,"nodeId":"FACT106-046","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-046"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Mogao Caves","stem":"Which forms of art are particularly associated with the Mogao Caves?","choices":["Modern welded-steel installations","Baroque oil ceilings alone","Gothic stained glass alone","Buddhist wall paintings and sculpture","Roman floor mosaics alone"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":"Buddhist wall paintings and sculpture","explanation":"Wall paintings and sculpture preserve many centuries of Buddhist art along the Silk Road. The caves also connect art history with manuscripts and intercultural exchange.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/440/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Mogao Caves","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which forms of art are particularly associated with the Mogao Caves?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-23"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-23"],"fingerprint":"a030f34202b5b2f53411ff53"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-047","qid":106047,"nodeId":"FACT106-047","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-047"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Longmen Grottoes","stem":"Which dynastic periods are especially important for Longmen’s Buddhist sculpture?","choices":["Ming and Qing only","Maurya and Gupta","Chola and Chandella","Achaemenid and Sasanian","Northern Wei and Tang"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":"Northern Wei and Tang","explanation":"Rock-cut Buddhist images reveal changing styles and patronage, especially under Northern Wei and Tang rule. Compare carved stone at Longmen with painted cave interiors at Mogao.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1003/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Longmen Grottoes","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which dynastic periods are especially important for Longmen’s Buddhist sculpture?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-24"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-24"],"fingerprint":"a171229755e6531af25875ff"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-048","qid":106048,"nodeId":"FACT106-048","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-048"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Longmen Grottoes","stem":"What is the principal artistic medium of the monumental Buddhist images at Longmen?","choices":["Rock-cut stone sculpture","Oil painting on canvas","Cast-iron architectural panels","Woven silk tapestry","Woodblock prints on paper"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Rock-cut stone sculpture","explanation":"Rock-cut Buddhist images reveal changing styles and patronage, especially under Northern Wei and Tang rule. Compare carved stone at Longmen with painted cave interiors at Mogao.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1003/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Longmen Grottoes","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"What is the principal artistic medium of the monumental Buddhist images at Longmen?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:WORLD106-24"],"workIds95":["WORLD106-24"],"fingerprint":"be7978ba8bccde2e5f168e69"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-049","qid":106049,"nodeId":"FACT106-049","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-049","ART106-WAVE"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"The Great Wave off Kanagawa","stem":"Which technique was used for Hokusai’s Great Wave?","choices":["Bronze casting","Marble carving","Fresco","Woodblock printing","Oil painting"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":"Woodblock printing","explanation":"Look for tiny Mount Fuji beneath the crest and the three boats. This is a Japanese woodblock print from Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji, not an oil painting; size and framing make the mountain seem vulnerable.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/56353","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"The Great Wave off Kanagawa","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which technique was used for Hokusai’s Great Wave?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:ART106-WAVE"],"workIds95":["ART106-WAVE"],"fingerprint":"d7087db1545950c41254d690","imageId":"ART106-WAVE","visualSkill":"context_relation"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-050","qid":106050,"nodeId":"FACT106-050","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-050","ART106-WAVE"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"The Great Wave off Kanagawa","stem":"Which mountain appears beneath the wave’s crest in this print?","choices":["Mount Everest","Mount Olympus","Mount Vesuvius","Mount Fuji","Mount Kilimanjaro"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":"Mount Fuji","explanation":"Look for tiny Mount Fuji beneath the crest and the three boats. This is a Japanese woodblock print from Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji, not an oil painting; size and framing make the mountain seem vulnerable.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/56353","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"The Great Wave off Kanagawa","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which mountain appears beneath the wave’s crest in this print?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:ART106-WAVE"],"workIds95":["ART106-WAVE"],"fingerprint":"270a2328db73dac9c427cef0","imageId":"ART106-WAVE","visualSkill":"context_relation"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-051","qid":106051,"nodeId":"FACT106-051","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-051","ART106-SHIVA"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"Shiva as Lord of Dance (Nataraja)","stem":"Which deity is represented here as Lord of Dance?","choices":["Shiva","Ganesha","Surya","Vishnu","Indra"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Shiva","explanation":"The dancing Shiva combines movement with balance. A drum evokes creation and a flame destruction; the figure underfoot represents illusion. The copper-alloy sculpture belongs to a Hindu context, unlike a Buddha image.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39329","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Shiva as Lord of Dance (Nataraja)","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which deity is represented here as Lord of Dance?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:ART106-SHIVA"],"workIds95":["ART106-SHIVA"],"fingerprint":"0a2bebeabd378f4cf2d45553","imageId":"ART106-SHIVA","visualSkill":"context_relation"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-052","qid":106052,"nodeId":"FACT106-052","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-052","ART106-SHIVA"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"Shiva as Lord of Dance (Nataraja)","stem":"In this Nataraja image, what do drum and flame evoke?","choices":["Land and sea transport","Summer and winter only","Creation and destruction","Writing and arithmetic","Military rank and wealth"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Creation and destruction","explanation":"The dancing Shiva combines movement with balance. A drum evokes creation and a flame destruction; the figure underfoot represents illusion. The copper-alloy sculpture belongs to a Hindu context, unlike a Buddha image.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39329","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Shiva as Lord of Dance (Nataraja)","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"In this Nataraja image, what do drum and flame evoke?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:ART106-SHIVA"],"workIds95":["ART106-SHIVA"],"fingerprint":"acc35e2d62be66bea399b95f","imageId":"ART106-SHIVA","visualSkill":"context_relation"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-053","qid":106053,"nodeId":"FACT106-053","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-053","ART106-BENIN"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"Plaque with equestrian oba and attendants","stem":"What does the relative scale of the king and attendants communicate in this Benin plaque?","choices":["The order in which the figures were cast","Linear perspective distance","Hierarchy of rank","Exact physical height","Their biological ages"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Hierarchy of rank","explanation":"Compare the scale of the king with his attendants: relative size communicates rank. This is cast brass court art from Benin in present-day Nigeria. The wider tradition was dispersed through the British looting of the palace in 1897; provenance is part of its history.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/310752","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Plaque with equestrian oba and attendants","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"What does the relative scale of the king and attendants communicate in this Benin plaque?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:ART106-BENIN"],"workIds95":["ART106-BENIN"],"fingerprint":"c4fb59395d9a20abb529eca2","imageId":"ART106-BENIN","visualSkill":"context_relation"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-054","qid":106054,"nodeId":"FACT106-054","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-054","ART106-BENIN"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"Plaque with equestrian oba and attendants","stem":"What is the material of this court plaque from Benin?","choices":["Papyrus","Silk","Porcelain","Brass","Marble"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":"Brass","explanation":"Compare the scale of the king with his attendants: relative size communicates rank. This is cast brass court art from Benin in present-day Nigeria. The wider tradition was dispersed through the British looting of the palace in 1897; provenance is part of its history.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/310752","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Plaque with equestrian oba and attendants","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"What is the material of this court plaque from Benin?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:ART106-BENIN"],"workIds95":["ART106-BENIN"],"fingerprint":"d9e69b82989d6a720cb68ae4","imageId":"ART106-BENIN","visualSkill":"context_relation"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-055","qid":106055,"nodeId":"FACT106-055","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-055","ART106-BUDDHA"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"Buddha, dated 680","stem":"This Buddha is dated 680. Which Chinese dynasty provides its historical context?","choices":["Ming","Qing","Tang","Shang","Yuan"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Tang","explanation":"This marble Buddha is explicitly dated 680 in the collection record. Separate the date of this object from the much longer Tang dynasty (618–907); a dynasty is context, not an exact date for every work.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39756","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Buddha, dated 680","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"This Buddha is dated 680. Which Chinese dynasty provides its historical context?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:ART106-BUDDHA"],"workIds95":["ART106-BUDDHA"],"fingerprint":"29008634b087c90f7024fa22","imageId":"ART106-BUDDHA","visualSkill":"context_relation"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-056","qid":106056,"nodeId":"FACT106-056","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-056","ART106-BUDDHA"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"Buddha, dated 680","stem":"Which date belongs to this particular Buddha, rather than to the whole dynasty?","choices":["1368–1644","618–907","680","1644–1911","206 BCE–220 CE"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"680","explanation":"This marble Buddha is explicitly dated 680 in the collection record. Separate the date of this object from the much longer Tang dynasty (618–907); a dynasty is context, not an exact date for every work.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39756","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Buddha, dated 680","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which date belongs to this particular Buddha, rather than to the whole dynasty?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:ART106-BUDDHA"],"workIds95":["ART106-BUDDHA"],"fingerprint":"d1ce5877d80758b23477d741","imageId":"ART106-BUDDHA","visualSkill":"context_relation"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-057","qid":106057,"nodeId":"FACT106-057","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-057","ART106-IZNIK"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"Bowl with Variation of 'Baba Nakkas' Design","stem":"Which material and decorative process describe this Iznik-attributed bowl?","choices":["Stonepaste with blue painting under transparent glaze","Woven wool with silk embroidery","Carved marble with gold leaf","Oil paint on canvas","Cast brass with stamped lettering"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Stonepaste with blue painting under transparent glaze","explanation":"Blue and white floral decoration lies beneath a transparent glaze on stonepaste. The attribution is to Iznik; the design-family name does not securely identify the individual potter. Notice how ornament follows the curved vessel.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/448664","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Bowl with Variation of 'Baba Nakkas' Design","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which material and decorative process describe this Iznik-attributed bowl?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:ART106-IZNIK"],"workIds95":["ART106-IZNIK"],"fingerprint":"e2d4927dffa6de3cf02db709","imageId":"ART106-IZNIK","visualSkill":"context_relation"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-058","qid":106058,"nodeId":"FACT106-058","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-058","ART106-IZNIK"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"Bowl with Variation of 'Baba Nakkas' Design","stem":"Why is it misleading to treat “Baba Nakkas” as a securely identified maker of this bowl?","choices":["The record identifies Hokusai as the maker","The name describes a design group, not a secure individual attribution","It names an Inca ruler","It is the chemical name for marble","The bowl is a twentieth-century photograph"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"The name describes a design group, not a secure individual attribution","explanation":"Blue and white floral decoration lies beneath a transparent glaze on stonepaste. The attribution is to Iznik; the design-family name does not securely identify the individual potter. Notice how ornament follows the curved vessel.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/448664","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Bowl with Variation of 'Baba Nakkas' Design","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Why is it misleading to treat “Baba Nakkas” as a securely identified maker of this bowl?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:ART106-IZNIK"],"workIds95":["ART106-IZNIK"],"fingerprint":"9383995e190a0e93658c6261","imageId":"ART106-IZNIK","visualSkill":"context_relation"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-059","qid":106059,"nodeId":"FACT106-059","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-059","ART106-MOCHE"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"Stirrup Spout Bottle with Portrait Head","stem":"Which culture produced this portrait-head vessel?","choices":["Maya","Edo of Benin","Moche","Inca","Khmer"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Moche","explanation":"The portrait head is also a ceramic vessel, with a stirrup-shaped spout joining its passages. The Moche context predates the Inca Empire by centuries; shared geography does not mean a single culture or period.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/308531","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"topic74":"Stirrup Spout Bottle with Portrait Head","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"Which culture produced this portrait-head vessel?","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["world106:ART106-MOCHE"],"workIds95":["ART106-MOCHE"],"fingerprint":"63e920b25005eb0a38db8397","imageId":"ART106-MOCHE","visualSkill":"context_relation"},{"id":"ARCHED-WORLD106-060","qid":106060,"nodeId":"FACT106-060","relatedNodeIds":["FACT106-060","ART106-MOCHE"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"Stirrup Spout Bottle with Portrait Head","stem":"Which statement correctly relates this Moche vessel to Inca history?","choices":["It dates to the same building campaign as Machu Picchu","It predates the Inca Empire by centuries","It was commissioned by a Spanish viceroy","It was produced after Peru’s independence","It is a Roman import to Peru"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"It predates the Inca Empire by centuries","explanation":"The portrait head is also a ceramic vessel, with a stirrup-shaped spout joining its passages. The Moche context predates the Inca Empire by centuries; shared geography does not mean a single culture or period.","format":"single_choice","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/308531","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"world-knowledge-v106","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_r